veral maps whose order is large relative to the size of the set, implying that a natural CSP is unlikely. Sometimes we can prove facts about the orbit sizes either as a corollary to the homomesy or by the technique used to prove homomesy.\n\nMany of the actions we describe are products of much simpler ones. Among these, we consider maps defined as products of simple \"toggling\" involutions. These come from the Striker's theory of generalized toggle groups, an active area of research in dynamical algebraic combinatorics. Several known instances of homomesy have been discovered for elements of toggle groups. While the individual toggles have order two, the order of a composition of several toggles is more difficult to analyze. We also consider an action of \"whirling,\" due to Propp, that can be defined for any family of functions between finite sets. This action is also the composition of simpler ones.\n\n## Recommended Citation\n\nJoseph, Michael J., \"Toggling Involutions and Homomesies for Maps on Finite Sets, Noncrossing Partitions, and Independent Sets of Path Graphs\" (2017). Doctoral Dissertations. 1424.\nhttps://digitalcommons.lib.uconn.edu/dissertations/1424\n\nDownload--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParking Functions | 19 | Handbook of Enumerative Combinatorics | Cathe (https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.1201/b18255-19/parking-functions-catherine-yan)\nciteturn9740search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; 13.1 Introduction The notion of parking functions was introduced by Konheim and Weiss [53] as a colorful way to describe their work on computer storage. ... The cars enter the street one at a time in the order C1,C2, . . . ,Cn. ... The sequence (a1, . . . ,an) is called a parking function of length n if all the cars can park, i.e., no car leaves the street.\n## ABSTRACT\n\nReferences . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 888\n\n13.1 Introduction The notion of parking functions was introduced by Konheim and Weiss [53] as a colorful way to describe their work on computer storage. The parking problem can be stated as follows. There are n cars C1, . . . ,Cn that want to park on a one-way street with ordered parking spaces 0,1, . . . ,n−1. Each car Ci has a preferred space ai. The cars enter the street one at a time in the order C1,C2, . . . ,Cn. A car tries to park in its preferred space. If that space is occupied, then it parks in the next available space. If there is no space then the car leaves the street. The sequence (a1, . . . ,an) is called a parking function of length n if all the cars can park, i.e., no car leaves the street.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n- FindStat (https://www.findstat.org/MapsDatabase/Mp00298/)\nciteturn9740search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; The unique parking function obtained by computing successive differences to obtain a Prüfer code, then prepending 0 and cyclically shifting.Pollak's map [1] sends a parking function $(p_1,\\dots,p_n)$ to the Prüfer code $(c_1,\\dots,c_{n-1})$ with $c_i = p_{i+1} - p_i \\pmod{n+1}$.\n\nIdentifier\n\nMp00298: Parking functions —repeats to leading⟶ Parking functions\n\n\nIdentities\n\nclick to show experimental identities (only identities of compositions of up to three maps are shown)\n\nInverses\nMp00301leading to repeats ∘ Mp00298repeats to leading = id : Parking functions ⟶ Parking functions\nMp00298repeats to leading ∘ Mp00301leading to repeats = id : Parking functions ⟶ Parking functions\nCommutation relations\nMp00298repeats to leading ∘ Mp00303complement = Mp00303complement ∘ Mp00298repeats to leading : Parking functions ⟶ Parking functions\nIntertwining relations\nMp00056to Dyck path ∘ Mp00298repeats to leading ∘ Mp00320reverse = Mp00121Cori-Le Borgne involution ∘ Mp00056to Dyck path ∘ Mp00298repeats to leading : Pa